# Brent minimization

Brent's method minimizes a function of one scalar variable on a finite
interval. It combines parabolic interpolation with a golden-section fallback
and needs only objective values.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/brent/struct.Brent.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                              |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A deterministic scalar function                          |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`                                           |
  | Search      | Local interval reduction, ideally on a unimodal function  |
  | Constraints | Scalar box bounds with finite `lower < upper`             |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features           |

## When to use it

Consider Brent when you can isolate a minimum in an interval and do not have
an inexpensive derivative. On a smooth function, interpolation often reduces
the number of evaluations compared with [golden-section
search](../golden-section/). If reliable first derivatives are cheap, compare
[Brent with derivatives](../brent-derivative/).

Use an interval where the function decreases toward a minimum and increases
afterward, or is monotone toward a boundary minimum. Several local minima,
noise, or broad flat regions can make interval reduction discard a better
region. A narrow final interval does not certify global optimality.

For a scalar equation `f(x) = 0`, see [Brent root
finding](../../root-finding/brent/).

## How it works

[Brent (1973)](#ref-brent-1973), Chapter 5, combines successive parabolic
interpolation with golden-section search. Basin follows the form in
[Press et al. (1992)](#ref-press-etal-1992), §10.2.

The solver retains a best point and two other sampled points. It proposes
the minimum of a parabola through their objective values, accepting the step
only when it stays inside the interval and is small enough relative to recent
steps. Otherwise, it takes a golden-section step toward the larger side of
the interval.

One new objective evaluation updates the interval and interpolation history.
A minimum displacement based on the position tolerances keeps probes apart
at the requested resolution.

## Example

The quadratic `(x - 2)^2` has its minimum at `2`. This example first discovers
an interval with `MinimumBracketer`, then declares its outer endpoints as
problem-side bounds and uses the middle point to seed Brent.

<!-- compile: solver-brent -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, Brent, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Executor, MinimumBracketer, PointState, TerminationCode,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic {
    lower: f64,
    upper: f64,
}

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = f64;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &f64) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x - 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Constraints for Quadratic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<f64>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let search = MinimumBracketer::new(0.0, 0.5, 1.0)
        .bracket(|x: f64| Ok::<_, Infallible>((x - 2.0).powi(2)))
        .unwrap();
    assert!(search.bracketed());

    let (lower, middle, upper) = search.bracket();
    let solver = Brent::new().with_absolute_position_tolerance(1e-10);
    let result = Executor::new(
        Quadratic { lower, upper },
        solver,
        PointState::new(middle),
    )
    .max_iter(100)
    .run()
    .unwrap();

    assert_eq!(result.report.code(), TerminationCode::SolverConverged);
    assert!((result.best_param() - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-12);
    let total_evaluations = search.function_evals() + result.counts.cost_evals;
    assert!(total_evaluations > result.counts.cost_evals);
}
```

If you already know the interval, declare its endpoints directly and supply
an interior seed through `PointState`. Scalar minimizers use `Executor::new`
with an explicit state.

## Practical considerations

### Supply a finite interval inside the objective's domain

The complete constraint declaration must be `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<F>>`
with both endpoints present. Linear and nonlinear blocks are unsupported.
The endpoints must be finite and strictly ordered. Use a scale where interval
differences and midpoint calculations remain finite, and return finite
objective values at every probe.

Brent clamps the initial seed to the interval. If it lands on an endpoint,
the solver moves it to a golden-section interior point before evaluating.
An endpoint minimum is approached from within the interval; the solver does
not separately evaluate both endpoints.

[`MinimumBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/minimum/struct.MinimumBracketer.html)
searches for an interior minimum bracket, with optional domain limits. Check
`bracketed()` before using its output. A boundary minimum can exhaust the
search limits without producing an interior bracket; for a known monotone
objective, supply the interval directly.

### Choose position accuracy

`with_absolute_position_tolerance` defaults to `1e-12`, and
`with_relative_position_tolerance` defaults to `sqrt(ε)` for the scalar type.
For the retained best point `x`, current interval `[a, b]`, and midpoint `m`,
the native test is `abs(x - m) + (b - a) / 2 <= 2 * tol`, where
`tol = relative * abs(x) + absolute`.

Both settings must be finite and strictly positive; invalid values panic.
They also control the minimum trial displacement. Choose tolerances that
match the scale and precision of the objective, especially with `f32`.
Shrinking an interval cannot recover position information lost when nearby
objective values round to the same number.

### Read the best point and termination report

The current `param()` and `cost()` describe the most recent probe, which can
be worse than an earlier point. Use `best_param()` and `best_cost()` for the
lowest recorded objective, particularly after an iteration or evaluation
limit. Set budgets on the executor with `max_iter` or `max_cost_evals`, and
check `result.report` to distinguish native convergence from a budget stop.

Optional cost-change and step-change tolerances can stop the run before the
interval test. Those checks compare successive published probes and provide
different evidence from interval reduction. Brent publishes no gradient, so
gradient convergence settings are absent from its API.

## Implementation

Brent uses `PointState<F, F>`. Initialization evaluates one seed, and each
completed iteration requests one new cost. Automatic bracketing counts its
work separately, and the minimizer reevaluates its seed.

A fresh run resets interpolation history. An exact solver-and-state
checkpoint retains it with the evaluation counts. Scalar `f32` and `f64`
require no linear-algebra backend or optional feature and work on WASM.

